{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pvi4ydYj5XaF9mVC2eAeA1","type":"tool_result","content":"package worker\n\nimport \"strings\"\n\n// CommitType maps an issue's type:* label to a conventional-commit type:\n// type:feature is feat, type:bug is fix, and docs, chore, refactor and test\n// keep their names. Any other label, or none, is chore.\nfunc CommitType(labels []string) string {\n\tfor _, l := range labels {\n\t\tswitch strings.ToLower(l) {\n\t\tcase \"type:feature\":\n\t\t\treturn \"feat\"\n\t\tcase \"type:bug\":\n\t\t\treturn \"fix\"\n\t\tcase \"type:docs\":\n\t\t\treturn \"docs\"\n\t\tcase \"type:chore\":\n\t\t\treturn \"chore\"\n\t\tcase \"type:refactor\":\n\t\t\treturn \"refactor\"\n\t\tcase \"type:test\":\n\t\t\treturn \"test\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn \"chore\"\n}\n527-\n528-// push commits the guard's files to the branch through the code lane.\n529-func (jb *job) push(ctx context.Context, s step, v Verdict) (string, bool) {\n530-\td := jb.l.d\n531-\treq := committer.CodeRequest{\n532:\t\tIssue: jb.is.Number, Role: jb.role.Name, JobID: jb.sj.ID, Type: CommitType(jb.is.Labels),\n533-\t\tTitle: jb.is.Title, Body: jb.summary, Base: s.h.BaseSHA, Create: !jb.exists,\n534-\t}\n535-\tfor _, c := range v.Commit {\n536-\t\tcf := committer.CodeFile{Path: c.Path, Delete: c.Deleted}\n537-\t\tif !c.Deleted {\n--\n705-// marking it ready is the merger's. It reports false after finishing the job.\n706-func (jb *job) openPR(ctx context.Context, res *closeout.WorkerResult) bool {\n707-\td := jb.l.d\n708-\tn := jb.is.Number\n709-\tres.Reviewers = jb.reviewers(ctx)\n710:\ttitle, err := Title(CommitType(jb.is.Labels), jb.is.Title, n, jb.sj.ID)\n711-\tif err != nil {\n712-\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.pr_failed\", \"step\", \"title\", \"error\", err)\n713-\t\tjb.finish(ctx, jb.final, nil, jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"could not open the pull request\", false))\n714-\t\treturn false\n715-\t}\n37-\t}\n38-}\n39-\n40:func TestCommitType(t *testing.T) {\n41-\tfor _, tc := range []struct {\n42-\t\tlabels []string\n43-\t\twant   string\n44-\t}{\n45-\t\t{nil, \"chore\"},\n46-\t\t{[]string{\"type:feature\"}, \"feat\"},\n47-\t\t{[]string{\"area:x\", \"type:bug\"}, \"fix\"},\n48-\t\t{[]string{\"type:docs\"}, \"docs\"},\n49-\t\t{[]string{\"type:chore\"}, \"chore\"},\n50-\t\t{[]string{\"type:refactor\"}, \"refactor\"},\n51-\t\t{[]string{\"type:test\"}, \"test\"},\n52-\t\t{[]string{\"TYPE:Bug\"}, \"fix\"},\n53-\t\t{[]string{\"type:decision\"}, \"chore\"},\n54-\t} {\n55:\t\tif got := CommitType(tc.labels); got != tc.want {\n56:\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"CommitType(%v) = %q, want %q\", tc.labels, got, tc.want)\n57-\t\t}\n58-\t}\n59-}\n60-\n61-func rc(path string) Change { return Change{Path: path} }\n62-\n63-func TestCheck(t *testing.T) {\n64-\tbig := rc(\"big.txt\")\n65-\tbig.Size = MaxFileSize + 1\n66-\tbin := rc(\"img.png\")\n67-\tbin.Binary = true\n68-\tsym := rc(\"link\")\n69-\tsym.Kind = Symlink\n70-\tnested := rc(\"sub\")\n71-\tnested.Kind = NestedRepo\n72-\tother := rc(\"fifo\")\n73-\tother.Kind = Other\n74-\tknown := rc(\"a.txt\")\n75-\tknown.Known = []string{\"MODEL_KEY\"}\n76-\tpat := rc(\"b.txt\")\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n15:- **Workers** — every 5 min; implement the plan on `worker/<name>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR\n28:    G -->|\"ard:worker:ROLE\"| W[\"Worker<br/>branch, code, test, draft PR\"]\n42:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n60:    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n68:`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n75:| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n76:| `ard:review:<role>` | Worker close-out (on the PR) | This review is requested |\n79:| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n103:    plan.md              # triage roles except the lead: each owns one `##` section, replaced in place on a re-run; the worker follows it\n107:    worker-engineer.md         # worker: what changed, test results, open issues\n111:      worker-engineer-<ts>.log   # one per agent step; test logs stay on the host\n123:| `worker:*`, `review:*` | its role file only |\n125:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change.\n127:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n137:worker: engineer      # lead only: which worker role implements the plan\n152:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags.\n154:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n156:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n158:**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n213:Triage turns an issue into a `plan.md` a worker can execute, one role at a time, and writes only to `.ard/<issue#>/` on `main`.\n218:2. Wait for a free job slot (triage ranks below reviewers and workers), then claim the oldest match: add `ard:triage:<role>:running` and record the job (id, executor, start, deadline). Claiming runs in a watcher goroutine; at most one triage job per issue; under `--dry-run` the loop logs `triage.would_claim` and creates no job, slot or label.\n223:**The lead routes; the controller applies.** The lead writes `next: [triage:architect, triage:product-manager]` and `worker: engineer` in `triage-lead.md`. The controller adds the matching `ard:` labels only if each names a role directory that exists.\n225:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n227:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker's 1,000-line guard (XL is 1,000 lines or more). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n229:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\n231:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\n233:With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fc6db4e6-f32b-4dea-97b7-976a28d49ef2","uuid":"42797618-145b-4f75-a0a8-802f6a0e0388","timestamp":"2026-11-23T10:35:26.314Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package worker\n\nimport \"strings\"\n\n// CommitType maps an issue's type:* label to a conventional-commit type:\n// type:feature is feat, type:bug is fix, and docs, chore, refactor and test\n// keep their names. Any other label, or none, is chore.\nfunc CommitType(labels []string) string {\n\tfor _, l := range labels {\n\t\tswitch strings.ToLower(l) {\n\t\tcase \"type:feature\":\n\t\t\treturn \"feat\"\n\t\tcase \"type:bug\":\n\t\t\treturn \"fix\"\n\t\tcase \"type:docs\":\n\t\t\treturn \"docs\"\n\t\tcase \"type:chore\":\n\t\t\treturn \"chore\"\n\t\tcase \"type:refactor\":\n\t\t\treturn \"refactor\"\n\t\tcase \"type:test\":\n\t\t\treturn \"test\"\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn \"chore\"\n}\n527-\n528-// push commits the guard's files to the branch through the code lane.\n529-func (jb *job) push(ctx context.Context, s step, v Verdict) (string, bool) {\n530-\td := jb.l.d\n531-\treq := committer.CodeRequest{\n532:\t\tIssue: jb.is.Number, Role: jb.role.Name, JobID: jb.sj.ID, Type: CommitType(jb.is.Labels),\n533-\t\tTitle: jb.is.Title, Body: jb.summary, Base: s.h.BaseSHA, Create: !jb.exists,\n534-\t}\n535-\tfor _, c := range v.Commit {\n536-\t\tcf := committer.CodeFile{Path: c.Path, Delete: c.Deleted}\n537-\t\tif !c.Deleted {\n--\n705-// marking it ready is the merger's. It reports false after finishing the job.\n706-func (jb *job) openPR(ctx context.Context, res *closeout.WorkerResult) bool {\n707-\td := jb.l.d\n708-\tn := jb.is.Number\n709-\tres.Reviewers = jb.reviewers(ctx)\n710:\ttitle, err := Title(CommitType(jb.is.Labels), jb.is.Title, n, jb.sj.ID)\n711-\tif err != nil {\n712-\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.pr_failed\", \"step\", \"title\", \"error\", err)\n713-\t\tjb.finish(ctx, jb.final, nil, jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"could not open the pull request\", false))\n714-\t\treturn false\n715-\t}\n37-\t}\n38-}\n39-\n40:func TestCommitType(t *testing.T) {\n41-\tfor _, tc := range []struct {\n42-\t\tlabels []string\n43-\t\twant   string\n44-\t}{\n45-\t\t{nil, \"chore\"},\n46-\t\t{[]string{\"type:feature\"}, \"feat\"},\n47-\t\t{[]string{\"area:x\", \"type:bug\"}, \"fix\"},\n48-\t\t{[]string{\"type:docs\"}, \"docs\"},\n49-\t\t{[]string{\"type:chore\"}, \"chore\"},\n50-\t\t{[]string{\"type:refactor\"}, \"refactor\"},\n51-\t\t{[]string{\"type:test\"}, \"test\"},\n52-\t\t{[]string{\"TYPE:Bug\"}, \"fix\"},\n53-\t\t{[]string{\"type:decision\"}, \"chore\"},\n54-\t} {\n55:\t\tif got := CommitType(tc.labels); got != tc.want {\n56:\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"CommitType(%v) = %q, want %q\", tc.labels, got, tc.want)\n57-\t\t}\n58-\t}\n59-}\n60-\n61-func rc(path string) Change { return Change{Path: path} }\n62-\n63-func TestCheck(t *testing.T) {\n64-\tbig := rc(\"big.txt\")\n65-\tbig.Size = MaxFileSize + 1\n66-\tbin := rc(\"img.png\")\n67-\tbin.Binary = true\n68-\tsym := rc(\"link\")\n69-\tsym.Kind = Symlink\n70-\tnested := rc(\"sub\")\n71-\tnested.Kind = NestedRepo\n72-\tother := rc(\"fifo\")\n73-\tother.Kind = Other\n74-\tknown := rc(\"a.txt\")\n75-\tknown.Known = []string{\"MODEL_KEY\"}\n76-\tpat := rc(\"b.txt\")\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n15:- **Workers** — every 5 min; implement the plan on `worker/<name>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR\n28:    G -->|\"ard:worker:ROLE\"| W[\"Worker<br/>branch, code, test, draft PR\"]\n42:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n60:    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n68:`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n75:| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n76:| `ard:review:<role>` | Worker close-out (on the PR) | This review is requested |\n79:| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n85:Four more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:r